From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:07:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 181/182] fix(gastown): route a rejected bead back to the pool
 that built it (gcp-j1ao)

mol-refinery-patrol's reject paths (rebase-conflict reject and the handle-failures
step) and acceptance-check.sh rec_return_to_pool all hardcoded gc.routed_to to the
rig's default polecat pool. In a rig with more than one implementer pool, a rejected
bead built by a non-default pool (winnow-jkhs1, built by specialists.view) was queued
for a Go polecat that lacks that lane's skills and verification setup.

A rejected bead now goes back to its gc.execution_routed_to, which gc sling stamps with
the pool it routed the bead to, falling back to the rig's default polecat pool when the
stamp is empty (MAYOR ruling 7, option A). One rule everywhere:
(.[0].metadata."gc.execution_routed_to" // "") | tostring. merge-push.sh gains
resolve_reject_route for the scripts; the formula's two reject-update fences and the
verbatim refinery prompt copy read REJECT_ROUTE by the same rule. An unreadable bead
takes the default with a WARN on stderr. Both prose lines now say the fence runs in ONE
Bash call, because REJECT_ROUTE crosses statements and an empty one would orphan the
bead silently.

Applied verbatim from the verified artifact (carrier gcp-uc988, sha256 603cae39...).
Tests: test_refinery_acceptance_check.sh gains
case_record_reject_routes_to_the_slung_pool (22 -> 23 cases) and
test_refinery_reject_branch_survival.sh gains four route tests. Beads slung before the
stamp existed fall back to the default; no backfill.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
